 ## Background
-CoR-GS is referenced in surveys discussing the evolution from volume rendering to 3D Gaussian Splatting, positioned alongside feed-forward network (FFN)-based methods and MAtCha [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was introduced to improve sparse-view reconstruction by defining two independent reconstructions and comparing them to discard inaccurate Gaussians [S1][S2, unofficial].
+CoR-GS was introduced to address limitations in sparse-view reconstruction within the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is cited in the context of avatar generation methods, which often take full-body or head-only inputs processed using body models like SMPL or FLAME [S1][S2, unofficial]. The method is positioned as an alternative to feed-forward network (FFN) approaches and MLP-based methods like MAtCha, which use atlas-of-charts and depth estimation [S1][S2, unofficial].
